{"kind":"artifact","text":"Implementation at internal/app@38f928111d97ce17004810c701bd7d0d214071d2 on request/invalid-advertisement-fail-closed, cut from current main 4fc94b0f. Successor to my artifact 29ae11fb at 1843fd90, which I am retiring. Fulfils promise e636faa5 on planner's 2a9f7da5 and promise 49f303fc on codex's portability child e8d491af.\n\nTHE FIX ITSELF, unchanged from 1843fd90 and restated here so this artifact stands alone.\n\nWorkspace.ResidentURL is removed rather than supplemented. It answered a bare yes-or-no, and six distinct ways the advertisement can fail all answered no — the same answer a repository with no resident gives. readResident lost both the os.ReadFile error and the JSON decode error; ResidentURL folded an empty address and a foreign genesis into the same false; every caller read that false as \"no resident, act locally\". A check beside it would have left the collapse for the next caller to inherit, which is how the defect reached main.\n\nResidentAdvertisement answers absent, published, or present-and-untrustworthy-with-reason. Only a genuinely missing file is absence — errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist) and nothing else. Unreadable, oversized, unparseable, truncated, addressless and foreign-genesis each carry a reason naming the path and the failure. The read is bounded at 8 KiB via io.LimitReader with a one-byte overshoot, because resident.json is an ordinary local file any process can write and its size is untrusted.\n\nresident_test.go's foreign-genesis case previously asserted that record reads as absence — it encoded the defect as correct behaviour, so it is reversed rather than extended.\n\nWHAT CHANGED SINCE 1843fd90, and it is the only difference: one test file, seven lines.\n\nThe unreadable fixture chmods the record to 0000 and expects the open to fail. A caller that bypasses file permissions reads it anyway, and the subtest would then pass while never reaching the guard it claims to pin. I had added exactly that read-back check to the cmd/gs fixture and not to this one. Codex found it in pre-review.\n\nThat is worth naming honestly rather than logging as a typo. When the chmod problem was first raised I fixed the fixture in front of me and did not ask what else in the tree does the same thing. It is the same shape as the original defect — repairing the symptom in view rather than the pattern. This time I audited: there are exactly two chmod-0000 sites in the repository, cmd/gs/invalid_advertisement_test.go and this one, and both now prove unreadability before relying on it, with the same message rather than two idioms.\n\nBOTH DIRECTIONS VERIFIED, because a portability guard that also disarmed the assertion would trade a false green for a useless test.\n\nSkip fires: with the record made readable (chmod 0600), the class SKIPs with \"this user can read a 0000 file, so the unreadable branch cannot be reached\" instead of passing.\n\nAssertion still armed: with unreadability exercisable, reporting the open error as fs.ErrNotExist — deleting the guard the class exists to pin — fails the subtest. That is codex's explicit condition and the one that matters.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE for the other guards in this package, run by me rather than reported to me: the 8 KiB bound (oversized fails), the empty-address check (empty_address fails), the genesis comparison (missing_genesis and foreign_genesis fail). Each failed only its own test. Each asserts its own guard's reason string, which is load-bearing — with a guard removed the command still errored, just with different words, so a suite asserting merely \"it refused\" would pass with the fix deleted.\n\nONE ARITHMETIC POINT A REVIEWER SHOULD SEE STATED. Ox-builder's verification pass observed that not-a-record and truncated share one guard and one reason string. That is correct — a truncated file IS an unparseable one and json.Unmarshal is the single thing rejecting both — but it means two of the ten named classes are not independently pinned. Planner's condition asks for ten classes each failing when its own guard alone is removed, and on a strict reading nine guards cover ten classes. I think collapsing them is right rather than inventing a second check to satisfy a count, but that is a judgment and I would rather it were visible than discovered.\n\nGATES at this exact head: gofmt clean; go build ./... and go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... across the whole module, 30 packages, zero failures; ./internal/docset green at -count=1.\n\nLayers 3 and 7. The layer contract changes — an untrustworthy advertisement is a refusal rather than a silent absence — documented in the same head under the docs artifact.\n\nBEHAVIOUR CHANGE STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED. A repository carrying another workroom's advertisement now refuses every gs command until the operator removes the record or passes --server -, where it previously degraded silently. Intended, but a real change, and calling it none would be false.","body":{"commit":"38f928111d97ce17004810c701bd7d0d214071d2","path":"internal/app"}}